title a guard shaped by the clock -- a red that heals itself by breakfast
prompt the fleet baton -- BAKERY's lane, core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first
rota lap 4117, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees. Threshold plus standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. The row's question is what must stop and what must be cut, and it aimed this lap: the cold open's red was read before any constructive work opened, the root was found rather than the symptom, and the cut was made at exactly one depth -- a capability on the roster, no change to the guard and none to its scan.
think Opened on the card whole, then fleet_round_open.sh, then the cold roster. The open answered `guards_red=2` and the two reds shared one root: `rota_declared` refuses, and `standing_equipment` reds because it reads its own roster's red.
think Diagnosis in one command rather than by reading. `rota_declared_scan.sh` counts how many of TODAY's session logs declare the rota row they read; run against yesterday's shelf it answers `logs=127 rota_field=25 prose_only=59 silent=43`, and against today's it refuses `no day shelf`. So the fault is the calendar, not the census -- and today is 20260907 with no shelf yet because this lap is the day's first.
think That makes it a red every ship meets at every midnight, and the cost is not the red itself. A red guard withholds the roster receipt, so `--scoped` refuses and every ship pays a FULL cold pass; this one ran 961s of guard time across 158 guards.
think The root, and it was already named one arm over. `%493` seated `capability seed_projection` for exactly this shape -- a guard reddening on an environment fact -- and its own words settle the direction: the scan's refusal is right (REDS %170), and the roster is where the environment fact belongs. A capability is a cadence rather than an exemption because absence is announced by name on every pass.
think Weighed the other repair and refused it. Making the scan report zeros instead of refusing would be REDS %514's fault exactly, booked one day earlier in a different room: two comment meters read empty as zero and both sat AT their ceilings, where a silent zero reads as their largest sweep. Silence and cleanliness read alike, so the scan keeps its refusal.
think The probe asks the guard's own question in BOTH halves. The scan refuses twice -- once for a missing shelf, once for a shelf holding no TRACKED log -- and a probe answering only the first would read `present` on a shelf whose only log is still untracked, which is a probe and its guard disagreeing about the same fact. That second half is planted separately in the pen.
think Unknown is real here where `seed_projection` has none. `git` is a tool that can go missing, and reading its absence as `absent` would SKIP the guard on a bench where nobody positively read anything -- so a missing git answers `unknown`, and unknown RUNS. That is the safety direction the runner's own header names.
think The unknown leg cannot be planted the way a missing bwrap can, because the runner reads the stash, the index and the tree with git -- a PATH without it answers for a reason that is not the probe. Asserted against the source instead, which is the precedent the jail arm already set for a leg the plants cannot reach.
obs One thing was found beside the repair and it was worth the minute: `sow_allow_reach`'s own capability comment stood 37 lines below its row, directly under `rota_declared` -- where the line this repair adds would have read as annotating the wrong guard. Moved to the row it has always been about.
obs The pen needed the same stand-in `rishi` the shared pen carries, or `alpha` reds and every count reads for the wrong reason. Two cases answered no until that was seen, which is the control doing its job on its own author.
obs Running the guard BY NAME re-recorded the red, because a named run is unfiltered by capability on purpose. The elder card row is dropped by a normal pass instead -- `%493`'s second half, built for exactly this.
obs FOUR rebases in one send renumbered this lap's row THREE times -- 529 to 530 to 532 to 533 -- and withdrew its fold twice, and the third renumber taught something the first two did not. The first two moved because a peer had bound the number with an EARLIER stamp. The third moved the other way: the peer's `%532` is stamped `20260907.005950` against this row's `20260907.003911`, so by stamp alone this row had the better claim -- and it moved anyway, because theirs was already PUBLISHED on the anointed spine and this one was not. **Derived-spine rule 3 outranks the stamp ordering**, and the two can point opposite ways; the stamp decides who keeps a number among rows still unshared, and publication decides it against anything local, however early. Two folds withdrawn beside that: %518 was folded by a peer an hour before mine, and then %523 was too, with two rows beside it, so the shelf written for a pair became a shelf for one and then no shelf at all. Every one of those moves cost one command, because the card, the commit body and this log cite the STAMP -- which is derived-spine rule 4 paying for itself on the lap that used it.
obs A daily red is worse than a standing one, and that is the reading this row adds to %493's. It heals itself an hour later, so it looks like somebody's fault and then vanishes, and nobody books what is gone by breakfast.
obs The link wall named by the thread's own recommend line stays booked as `%524` and was NOT taken. Reds first: the cold open's red books the allocation before constructive work opens, and this lap's finding was the red.
obs The verify pass found a SECOND instance of the same shape, and it was a peer's. `nib_honesty` read `gone=1` on `d8bb3f5ed2` -- a commit a peer tagged in their own tree and cited by hash in the shared card, which no other clone can resolve. It arrived on my rebase, present in upstream before this lap touched anything, so it was red on every ship that had pulled. Repaired by withholding the hash and keeping the fact: *tagged in that ship's own tree*. Named here and in the commit body rather than on the card, which stands 184 bytes under its ceiling.
obs Two roots, two laps, one lesson: a guard reading a red it did not cause reds beside it, so `standing_equipment` wore both. That is the roster telling the truth about itself rather than a second fault.
